  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14268926/assets/bda30909684d669063626459eca7ef17170bc39e0eada072a943957b0d6e4824.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a transitive relation defined on the set $A=\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma \\}?$",
  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a transitive relation. There are no pairs of vertices for which $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z.$\n\n- Digraph II represents a transitive relation. For any pair of arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ the arrow $x\\to z$ also belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $\\alpha \\to \\beta $ and $\\beta \\to \\gamma ,$ but has no arrow $\\alpha \\to \\gamma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
